--
dropped the body
--
__invoke_impl<_Partial<_Transform, ...>> and __invoke lose their bodies:
the call __f(__args...) of the _Partial closure object finds "no match for
symbol '_Partial'" (CBMC_CPP_SHOW_SUPPRESSED=1), as if the call named the
class's constructor rather than its member operator() template.  A
hand-written partial specialization called the same way works, so the cause
is in the remaining <ranges> context; not reduced further (cvise stopped at
its 2 h limit).  With libstdc++ 16 this is the drop of
cpp20_ranges_views_iota_transform_filter_drop (broken-libstdcxx16).
Findings 2026-10-27: with the GCC 16 headers, invoke_result_t<const
_Transform &, int (&)[4], const L &> computed directly in main is the
type of the two-argument call (transform_view), and views::transform(a,
f) works; only the call made from _Binder::_S_call in _Partial::operator()
gets _Partial as __invoke's result type, the type of the adaptor's
one-argument operator().  The program was reduced again (2026-10-27)
with a checker requiring declval<...>() calls and complete runs under
g++ 16, 13 and clang++ 18.
Findings 2026-10-28: inside the instantiated body of __invoke<_Transform,
int (&)[1], int &>, `typename decltype(_S_test<_Callable, _Args...>(0))
::type' is _Partial (the one-argument operator()), while the same with the
types spelled out, and `decltype(_Callable()(declval<_Args>()...))', are
the two-argument result; the declared return type of __invoke is right.
Header-free reconstructions of this shape do not fail.  Found on the way
and fixed: partial ordering with function parameter packs (ce6cf4a620),
variable template specializations as lvalues (37762b428d), the address of
a constexpr variable (9906f445dd).
